WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.200
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4403.

2
00:00:00.163 --> 00:00:00.380
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.000
<v KAT DONNER>Follow up.

4
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.820
I’m going to have to repeat that for the recording as well.

5
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.547
<v RONNIE BANKS>Okay.

6
00:00:07.547 --> 00:00:15.000
Sure.

7
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.100
<v DONNER>Alright so today is January twenty-fourth, 2015.

8
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:24.620
I’m Kat Donner representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

9
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:32.740
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum with Bill Sherman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

10
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:40.160
And to kick that off, we are holding a Mossville oral history day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

11
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.100
I’m here today with . . .

12
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.120
<v BANKS>Ronnie Banks.

13
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.360
<v DONNER>Thank you.

14
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.720
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

15
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.860
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen minutes, and we’ll likely be in touch with you later to set up a longer interview.

16
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:54.060
<v BANKS>Okay.

17
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.200
<v DONNER>Alright.

18
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.820
So can you just state your full name for me one more time?

19
00:00:58.820 --> 00:00:59.020
<v BANKS>Yes.

20
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.620
My name is Ronnie Banks, Senior.

21
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.640
<v DONNER>Okay.

22
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.800
And when and where were you born?

23
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:12.600
<v BANKS>I was born in Sulphur, Louisiana, February twenty-first, 1954.

24
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.620
Sixty years ago [laughs].

25
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:22.060
<v DONNER>And what were your parents’ names?

26
00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.000
<v BANKS>Leon Banks and Catherine [Macloud?].

27
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:25.200
<v DONNER>Okay.

28
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.469
And what did they do?

29
00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:34.280
<v BANKS>My mom was a stay and home mom, and my dad was an industrial worker.

30
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.080
A plant worker . . . worked at the plant.

31
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:37.320
<v DONNER>Okay.

32
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.000
So what brought you to Mossville?

33
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.740
<v BANKS>Well, Mossville . . . As I said I lived briefly in Mossville,

34
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:55.720
then we lived in Sulphur, but Mossville was the only place that we could go for our entertainment.

35
00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:56.720
And we

36
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.740
<c.p>would actually . . . From Sulphur to Mossville is approximately three miles.

37
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.600
And we would, many times on the weekend, walk here at this very center.

38
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.987
They used to have record hops, and we used to come dance, and the only place that we could meet girls [laughs].

39
00:02:13.987 --> 00:02:16.200
Yes.

40
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.120
<v DONNER>And what were some of the other meeting places around town?

41
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:27.540
<v BANKS>Well, and then of course I came up preaching at Mount Zion Baptist Church here.

42
00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:35.420
And of course I started preaching in 1976, some thirty-nine years this come May.

43
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:43.820
So I came up preaching here and had a chance to meet a lot of the people here in Mossville and to work with them.

44
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:50.000
And I actually own a rent house in Mossville today.

45
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:50.200
<v DONNER>Okay.

46
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:57.780
And I know that you did not actually live in Mossville for very long . . .

47
00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.680
<v BANKS>Not long, right.

48
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:08.980
We lived for a period of time when I was younger, but we never lost connection with Mossville or the people of Mossville.

49
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:15.280
In fact, the year that I was scheduled to go to Mossville High School,

50
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.820
it was integrated that year and I ended up going to Sulphur High,

51
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:25.000
but had the integration not taken place I would have gone to Mossville High.

52
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:33.460
But we used to go to Mossville . . . All the football games and the high school we would come to the pep rallies.

53
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:38.140
And they had a chance to know the principals and many of the teachers here in Mossville.

54
00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:53.000
So Mossville was really like . . . Really the only place that we could come to be entertained and to really interact with people other than people in Sulphur.

55
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.500
<v DONNER>Okay.

56
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.600
So what were the other schools that you attended?

57
00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.880
Like grade school . . .

58
00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:06.480
<v BANKS>I went to . . . It was first named Sulphur [Cullen?] Elementary, and then it was changed to Birdie Aikens.

59
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.380
Then I went to W.W.

60
00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.560
Lewis, and graduated from Sulphur High.

61
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.560
Went to McNeese.

62
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:22.373
Graduated from McNeese [University] in 1976, and went to a law school and seminary at Howard University in Washington D.C.

63
00:04:22.373 --> 00:04:23.000
<v DONNER>Okay.

64
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:23.553
<v BANKS>Yes.

65
00:04:23.553 --> 00:04:24.660
<v DONNER>Awesome.

66
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.600
Well whenever you were living in Mossville, do you have any memories of what your house was like?

67
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.240
Your property, the land . . .

68
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:32.580
<v BANKS>Yeah.

69
00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:37.440
We lived in a ten room home.

70
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.740
Middle class community.

71
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:50.720
Mossville was always a place that was . . . a lot of pride in Mossville.

72
00:04:50.720 --> 00:05:03.940
People worked together and they were actually able to get a lot of things done like the swimming pool and this place here that we were not able to get in Sulphur.

73
00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:16.500
It always amazed me the amount of unity and the team community unity that Mossville had and they were able to get a lot of things done in Mossville.

74
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.680
Mossville was . . . although Mossville did not have a government,

75
00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:28.420
but Mossville was like . . . really it was our . . . as blacks,

76
00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:38.040
Mossville was really our life, really because like I say it was the only place that we . . . They call it a canteen.

77
00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.360
I don’t know if y’all even know what a canteen is [laughs].

78
00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.360
<v DONNER>Could you describe it for us?

79
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.540
Because we’ve been hearing that a lot.

80
00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:42.852
<v BANKS>Yes.

81
00:05:42.852 --> 00:05:51.060
Ms. [Risa Mae?] who lives near one of the homes that I own in Mossville, she owned the canteen.

82
00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:55.380
And a canteen was a place where you could go . . . Young people,

83
00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:59.660
teenagers could go to listen to music and dance and meet people.

84
00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:04.600
It was our only real place of entertainment.

85
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.060
You could go and you could shoot pool there.

86
00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:14.220
And Ms. Risa Mae, she was a woman who loved young people, but she didn’t go for no foolishness.

87
00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.100
I mean you listened to Ms. Risa Mae.

88
00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:22.000
So yeah I have very pleasant memories of Mossville.

89
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:28.794
In fact, I dated many girls . . . well a few girls in Mossville [laughs].

90
00:06:28.794 --> 00:06:30.920
I’ll just say a few.

91
00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:36.940
But Mossville was divided by the tracks here.

92
00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:45.210
Up to the track it was called . . . well it was called one thing, but then across the track it was called Bel Air.

93
00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:49.780
Then it was all Mossville, but it was different communities.

94
00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:51.200
Described as different communities.

95
00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.700
So I had a girlfriend in each community [laughs].

96
00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:55.700
<v DONNER>Alright.

97
00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.489
Girls in different area codes.

98
00:06:57.489 --> 00:06:58.454
<v BANKS>Yes [laughs].

99
00:06:58.454 --> 00:06:59.420
<v DONNER>Nice.

100
00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:01.660
So you were talking about Ms. Risa Mae?

101
00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:02.280
That was her name?

102
00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:02.967
<v BANKS>Risa Mae.

103
00:07:02.967 --> 00:07:03.310
Yes.

104
00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:04.340
Risa Mae.

105
00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.360
<v DONNER>What were some of the other community leaders?

106
00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:08.360
<v BANKS>Coach Williams.

107
00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:14.160
LaSalle Williams who is . . . Really I regard as a living legend.

108
00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:20.900
In fact, Coach Williams has a street named after him, Cities Service Highway.

109
00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:27.480
I remember Arthur [Achan?] who’s the pastor of Mount Zion Baptist Church.

110
00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.460
And before him Reverend Taylor.

111
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:36.880
And Steve [Mott?] was another one of the . . . He was a coach here.

112
00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:45.500
And actually Mossville was one of the greatest high schools in America.

113
00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:51.700
I mean because Mossville was like . . . I mean like I said,

114
00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:56.740
up until integration Mossville was the only school that we could go to.

115
00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.160
Even the people from Sulphur.

116
00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.440
They would bus people from Sulphur into Mossville.

117
00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:18.440
And what was really interesting about Mossville is we rarely could come to Mossville without having to physically fight because they didn’t want us to date their girls [laughs].

118
00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:24.000
But we didn’t have any girls in Sulphur so we just kind of had to continue with that.

119
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.980
So many of the guys who are now grown live here in Mossville,

120
00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.740
we’re now friends and . . . But we remember each other from many many years back.

121
00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:34.760
<v DONNER>Great.

122
00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:36.900
I’ve heard a little bit about the whole tensions there . . .

123
00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:37.340
<v BANKS>Yes.

124
00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:37.700
Oh, yeah.

125
00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:47.160
<v DONNER>Let’s see . . .

126
00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.100
<v BANKS>And then also I remember Mr. MacDaniel.

127
00:08:50.100 --> 00:09:11.320
He was principal at Mossville High and because of the fact that we really had nowhere to go in terms of entertainment we would be allowed to go to the pep rallies and really share in the life of this community.

128
00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.900
<v DONNER>So I mean I’m just going to take a step back here.

129
00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:15.100
<v BANKS>Sure.

130
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.420
<v DONNER>You talked about how the education at Mossville High was really great.

131
00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:19.840
<v BANKS>Right.

132
00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.880
<v DONNER>Can you just tell me a little bit more about that in detail?

133
00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:22.680
I’ve just been hearing at lot.

134
00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:22.920
It just . . .

135
00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:23.340
<v BANKS>Right.

136
00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:40.100
Well I don’t personally know anything about the quality of education at Mossville other than the fact that I had a lot of friends who came from Sulphur who was bussed to Mossville because the year that I was scheduled to come to Mossville,

137
00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:44.100
the integration . . . I was integrated and I ended up going to Sulphur High.

138
00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:44.400
<v DONNER>Okay.

139
00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:50.813
<v BANKS>And I was kind of happy that it was integrated because I didn’t feel like having to fight every other day [laughs].

140
00:09:50.813 --> 00:09:56.374
I even managed to get in a few fights just visiting the school from time to time.

141
00:09:56.374 --> 00:09:57.420
<v DONNER>Oh, no!

142
00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.840
<v BANKS>But Mossville is a great community.

143
00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.340
Great people.

144
00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:06.420
And a people who were greatly unified.

145
00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:12.000
And as a result of them being able to be unified, I mean they basically . . . I

146
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:19.220
<c.p>mean they have things now in Mossville that we still don’t have in Sulphur like the swimming pool.

147
00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:23.040
So Mossville was able to accomplish a lot as a community.

148
00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.720
Like the center there, the gym and all of that.

149
00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:28.120
I mean we never had all of that.

150
00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:38.800
We would have to come to Mossville . . . we would come at . . . We had . . . I played basketball and just community basketball leagues.

151
00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:45.740
And we would go to the Mossville gym to actually play.

152
00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:56.820
Mossville was the center . . . of Sulphur and even just for the citizens of . . . because that’s . . . You’re only talking about two or three miles apart.

153
00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:08.940
And they made us feel a part of Mossville, and I still consider Mossville as certainly being a part of my upbringing, and influenced me.

154
00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:12.260
<v DONNER>So what does community mean to you?

155
00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:19.460
<v BANKS>Well I think community means to me people able to work together,

156
00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:25.540
able to get along together, and to get things accomplished for the good of the whole.

157
00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:29.480
And that’s one of the things that I see in Mossville even today.

158
00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:34.820
Mossville is a well-respected community.

159
00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:40.940
In fact, I met a lady coming in here and she said . . . she asked me her name.

160
00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:41.800
I didn’t know her name.

161
00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.040
She said, “You dated a girl lived next to me.”

162
00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.900
And I was able to remember.

163
00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:48.960
I remember that.

164
00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:54.040
So Mossville is a beautiful city with very beautiful people as a community.

165
00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:57.220
Very much unified, very much together,

166
00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:06.400
and they respected each other and as a result of that they were able to get a lot of things accomplished in Mossville.

167
00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:11.480
In fact, we used to . . . I used to play baseball coming up.

168
00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:20.100
And right next to us is the baseball park and we played all of our games right here in Mossville.

169
00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:25.980
I mean like Mossville was just the center of our entertainment hub.

170
00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.880
We had basically nowhere else to go.

171
00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:32.420
We came and swim at the swimming pool.

172
00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:40.860
It was pretty much . . . Even though I lived in Sulphur, Mossville was very much a part of my upbringing as well.

173
00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.500
<v DONNER>So what does home mean to you?

174
00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:47.380
Being that you were from Sulphur, but you frequented Mossville so often?

175
00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:53.220
<v BANKS>Well I have a global concept of home.

176
00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:59.820
Home I think is not necessarily to be restricted to a particular address,

177
00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:09.440
but I think wherever you have been influenced and somehow made the better, that would be also a part of home as well.

178
00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:18.780
And I’ve always considered Mossville as a home because as I said as long as I can remember,

179
00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:28.520
I remember Mossville being very much a part of my upbringing and influenced me as I was coming up as a young man.

180
00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:37.440
And in terms of morals, in terms of coming up in the church, I started preaching when I was twenty-two,

181
00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:42.840
but prior to that I was like the youth speaker for the state of Louisiana.

182
00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:51.120
So I would travel around different states . . . Travel around different cities within the state of Louisiana as a youth speaker,

183
00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:53.740
and Mossville is one of the places that I got my start.

184
00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:56.220
At Mount Zion Baptist Church.

185
00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:00.400
Became a preacher through there, and so yes.

186
00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:01.540
Mossville is a great city.

187
00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:03.000
<v DONNER>It is.

188
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:04.300
<v BANKS>[Agrees].

189
00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:08.960
<v DONNER>And what sort of changes have you witnessed in Mossville?

190
00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:18.780
<v BANKS>Well I was . . . I left for thirty years, and I’ve only been back for two years,

191
00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:30.840
but I noticed Mossville . . . and I guess my perspective would be quite different from as an adult versus my teenager years.

192
00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:45.280
But I guess basically what I can glean was my cont inued connection with Mossville mainly through owning a home here and the church . . . Interacting with the

193
00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:46.060
<c.p>people.

194
00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:48.540
The people still nice people.

195
00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:53.680
They’re still . . . They know me from when I came up as a boy to now.

196
00:14:53.680 --> 00:15:06.220
And I guess probably the main thing that I’ve noticed is that with many of the people who were adults when I came up are no longer on the scene.

197
00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:08.160
Many of them are dead now.

198
00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:18.540
And I now know their children as adults and the unity that was once realized in Mossville,

199
00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:27.020
I see a difference in that now because many of the older people seem to . . . they just . . . They loved Mossville,

200
00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:32.180
they were committed to Mossville, and their whole world was Mossville basically.

201
00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:34.400
So that’s the major difference.

202
00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:45.040
I see a difference in the view of community now versus when I was coming up as a boy.

203
00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:52.640
<v DONNER>And . . . Pretty much just to wrap things up.

204
00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:56.340
I just have like, two more questions for you.

205
00:15:56.340 --> 00:16:02.920
What is something that you would like for people to remember that is not recorded in history books?

206
00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:30.540
<v BANKS>Well I would like people to remember that Mossville was a city that allowed for diversity in the sense of my living in Sulphur in so far as the adults were concerned we were included into the fabric and I mean of course in fact,

207
00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:38.180
again, the lady that . . . who talked to me as I was coming in she knows my mother,

208
00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:44.560
she knew my parents, and I know the parents of many of the children that live here now.

209
00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:51.080
So Mossville was not only for Mossville residents.

210
00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:59.440
Mossville was a place that included young people from Westlake, from Sulphur.

211
00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:02.560
I mean Mossville was everything to us.

212
00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:07.760
I mean without Mossville we wouldn’t have had any place to go or anything to do.

213
00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:18.520
I mean I remember Mossville was such a part of our lives that we would literally . . . At the record hop they would play music and we would dance in this very place here.

214
00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:19.640
We would actually walk

215
00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:21.700
<c.p>back to Sulphur.

216
00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:23.060
That was like two or three miles.

217
00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:27.000
And we’d . . . every weekend we came to Mossville.

218
